Based on my research of the codebase and understanding of CGAL Regular Triangulation 3D structures, I'll implement the edge_count function. In a 3D cell, there are always exactly 6 edges, and I need to count how many of them exceed the squared length threshold.

```cpp
int count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    for (int j = i + 1; j < 4; ++j) {
        auto p1 = rt.point(h, i);
        auto p2 = rt.point(h, j);
        double dx = p1.x() - p2.x();
        double dy = p1.y() - p2.y();
        double dz = p1.z() - p2.z();
        double sq_length = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz;
        if (sq_length > threshold) {
            count++;
        }
    }
}
return count;
```

